<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ekur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ekur, Sumerian for 'mountain house', was the temple of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the most important religious centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. Because Enlil was held to grant kingship, rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> sought recognition there and rebuilt the temple; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> built its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> around 2100 BCE. Its supposed destruction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> of Akkad is the subject of the literary text known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/curse-of-agade">Curse of Agade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ekur.jpg" alt="Ekur" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jasmine N. Walthall, U.S. Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
